Register both listeners against one `AbortController` signal and abort it from whichever handler settles the promise, so settling detaches both. In src/browser/helpers.ts:

```ts
export function promisifyRequest<T>(request: IDBRequest<T>): Promise<T> {
	return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
		// One shared IDBRequest drives a whole cursor walk, so each settled bridge
		// detaches both of its listeners instead of accumulating them per move.
		const listeners = new AbortController()
		request.addEventListener(
			'success',
			() => {
				listeners.abort()
				resolve(request.result)
			},
			{ signal: listeners.signal },
		)
		request.addEventListener(
			'error',
			() => {
				listeners.abort()
				reject(wrapError(request.error))
			},
			{ signal: listeners.signal },
		)
	})
}
```

Both arrows are anonymous callbacks passed directly as arguments, which § Functions and orchestration permits. Do not use `{ once: true }` alone: it removes only the listener that fired and leaves its sibling attached, so the growth stays linear. Add a case to tests/src/browser/IndexedDBCursor.test.ts that seeds at least 20 records and walks them with `drainCursor`, asserting every record is returned in key order, so the change is pinned against a behavioural regression across many re-arms.
